{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1535136-fall-damage","id":1535136,"title":"Fall damage","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-05-04T03:26:48-07:00","id":1535136,"title":"Fall damage","content":"Silly question but do wraiths take fall damage?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28439-quelsec","name":"quelsec","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"407"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-05T16:31:51-07:00","id":1535185,"title":"","content":"Do you mean by falling and taking damage when they land? I would think so. The general guideline is the Rule of Ouch. If it is something that would damage a regular person in the Skinlands, then it damages the Wraith's corpus. Of course if the Wraith has some means of flying like with the Phantom Wings power of Argos, then they could glide safely down or even stay in the air. Or if they had some other power that would justify them avoiding the damage.<br /> <br /> Now if we are talking about the Tempest instead of the Shadowlands, I imagine the &quot;physics&quot; of the Underworld to act differently as a &quot;falling&quot; Wraith might never hit &quot;ground&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-06T06:51:01-07:00","id":1535192,"title":"","content":"Also, if the fall ends with the wraiths hitting a Skinlands obstacle, they take 1 damage and go incorporeal. They probably shouldn't continue falling into the ground though. It's usually better to use the Hollywood rules of insubstantiality.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-06-20T05:11:57-07:00","id":1537688,"title":"","content":"I took my copy of WtO second edition in hand yesterday and was reading specifically this rules about losing corpus and the Rule of Ouch.<br /> So I want to add to this question to see if I understood it correctly.<br /> <br /> Let's say a wraith falls from the top of a skyscraper.<br /> <br /> The wraith splats on the asphalt down there without hitting anything in the way.<br /> It takes a single corpus damage.<br /> Is it correct?<br /> <br /> Now there is a sentence about the aggravated damage that makes me think in such kind of theoretically massive damage the ST can decree this damage is aggravated, but it would still be a single point I think.<br /> <br /> After taking the damage the wraith becomes incorporeal.<br /> Does it stay on the asphalt?<br /> Does it pass through and goes in the sewers?<br /> ST decision?<br /> The wraith's decision?<br /> I would go with the last because it sounds fun that the wraith hits the asphalt, takes damage, becomes incorporeal and decides slipping inside the sewer isn't a bad course of action.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Ever since the Followers of Set rebranded themselves as The Ministry, I can barely keep a straight face around them.&quot; - Ramona","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/15944-ravnos","name":"Ravnos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar15944_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-06-20T12:27:44-07:00","id":1537721,"title":"","content":"RAW, the ground is a skinlands object, so the rule of Ouch applies. The wraith takes a single point of damage and goes incorporeal. <br /> <br /> What happens next is tricky. It's not a rule, but most people instinctively use the Hollywood Rules of Insubstantiality. That is, incorporeal entities don't fall through the floor unless they want to. It's usually better for the narrative if we don't have to deal with that.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, Hollywood usually doesn't have the insubstantial creature smack into the floor at high speed, and narratively we should probably deal with that speed.<br /> <br /> Here are some of the possibilities starting with the least satisfying and moving up.<br /> <br /> 1) The Wraith continues falling until the first time his incorporeality runs out. This could potentially leave a Wraith buried in 50 ft of bedrock and unless he has Argos or some other way of getting out. This is pretty much a long slog to a Harrowing, with the Wraith taking a Corpus of damage every few turns until he finally runs out. In most cases this will drag the plot to a halt and be really unfun for the player.<br /> <br /> 2) The Wraith stops dead (no pun intended) on the pavement, with no severe consequences.<br /> <br /> 3) The Wraith is flattened on the pavement and must spend a round or two pulling himself together. This works great if you treat the art in Wraith as representative.<br /> <br /> 4) The Wraith is buried a foot or two down and must spend a round or two clambering out. <br /> <br /> 5) If there are levels below the ground (subways, sewers, etc), the Wraith goes through one or more of them but comes to a stop in an open area.<br /> <br /> 6) The Wraith slams into the ground, tears through and falls straight into the Tempest. <br /> <br /> 5 and 6 are the most satisfying to me, but I'd say 3-6 all work well enough and I'd choose which one to use based on whatever was best for the story in the moment.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}